# Independent verification of the two-branch compose onto fix/aarch64-mc-itx

date:   2026-08-08
host:   Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc:  1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14), release, DEFAULT features
        (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16, CHECKED DisjointMut, tracker armed).
        No `-C target-cpu=native`. NO `nice` on any timed run.
ref:    dav1d 1.5.4 (homebrew), measured in the SAME interleaved sweep, same
        session, both at `--framedelay 1` and at its shipping default.
tree:   verify/compose-2 = fix/aarch64-mc-itx @ 2c7c082
          + perf/tracker-halve  @ 464c7bd  (merge 7e4f600, clean)
          + perf/lr-itx-neon    @ 788d117  (merge 2718219, 2 conflicts, resolved)
          + 2832454 (this campaign: one new soundness gate, one doc correction)
        BOTH branches merged. Nothing was left out.
raw:    benchmarks/verify_compose2_gap_2026-08-08.tsv   (main, 160 rows)
        benchmarks/verify_compose2_attr_2026-08-08.tsv  (attribution, 125 rows)
harness: scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh + verify_gap_report.py, unchanged.
        One process per (arm, cell), run at 2 and 20 frames,
        `beta = (ms20 - ms2)/18` so process startup drops out. Arm order
        rotates per round. A round is DISCARDED and re-run whole if any
        foreign process exceeds 25% CPU. **foreign = 0 on every committed
        cell of both sweeps** (5 discards total, all re-run).

This records what an INDEPENDENT re-measurement found, not what the two branch
reports claimed. Where they differ, the difference is stated.


## HEADLINE: materially closer, still not near. Best cell 1.63x, worst 2.42x.

ms/frame, median of 5 rounds, foreign=0:

  vector            t     base  composed  dav1d_fd1  dav1d_def  comp/base  vs fd1  vs def
  v4k_8tile         1    401.2     401.0      246.4      246.3      1.000    1.63    1.63
  v4k_8tile         2    222.9     216.6      125.0      125.7      0.972    1.73    1.72
  v4k_8tile         4    130.4     116.3       65.6       63.9      0.892    1.77    1.82
  v4k_8tile         8    116.7      73.1       34.7       33.6      0.627    2.11    2.18
  v4k_8tile_10b     1    507.2     438.1      251.3      251.0      0.864    1.74    1.75
  v4k_8tile_10b     2    274.8     245.5      127.5      128.7      0.893    1.93    1.91
  v4k_8tile_10b     4    158.3     129.9       66.8       65.4      0.821    1.95    1.99
  v4k_8tile_10b     8    137.6      85.1       36.3       35.1      0.618    2.34    2.42

The ~1.3x bar is met at NO thread count on either depth. The gap did shrink at
7 of 8 cells (t=1 8bpc is a dead heat) and the shrink is large where it was
worst: t=8 goes 3.37x -> 2.11x (8bpc) and 3.79x -> 2.34x (10bpc).

dav1d's own default is within 4% of its `--framedelay 1` figure at every cell
(0.97-1.01x), so the like-for-like restriction is not what the remaining 2x is
made of.


## NO INVERSION ANYWHERE

  vector                  arm   t1->t2   t2->t4   t4->t8   t1->t8 speedup
  v4k_8tile              base    0.556    0.585    0.894            3.44x
  v4k_8tile               all    0.540    0.537    0.628            5.48x
  v4k_8tile         dav1d_fd1    0.507    0.524    0.529            7.11x
  v4k_8tile         dav1d_def    0.510    0.508    0.525            7.34x
  v4k_8tile_10b          base    0.542    0.576    0.869            3.69x
  v4k_8tile_10b           all    0.560    0.529    0.655            5.15x
  v4k_8tile_10b     dav1d_fd1    0.507    0.524    0.543            6.93x
  v4k_8tile_10b     dav1d_def    0.513    0.508    0.537            7.15x

Every step is < 1.000: no t=N -> t=2N cell is slower than its half. The base's
near-inversion at t=4 -> t=8 (0.894 / 0.869) is gone (0.628 / 0.655).

Scaling is now the SECOND problem, not the first: t=1 -> t=8 is 5.48x against
dav1d's 7.11x, but the t=1 ratio is already 1.63x. Even perfect scaling from
here would leave 1.63x.


## ATTRIBUTION — which branch bought what (5 arms, median of 5, foreign=0)

  vector            t     base      trk       lr  composed  dav1d_fd1  trk/base  lr/base  comp/base
  v1024             8     27.0     26.8     26.7      26.7       14.4     0.994    0.990      0.990
  v256              8      1.111    1.056    1.111     1.056      0.778    0.950    1.000      0.950
  v4k_1tile         8    362.7    373.8    359.6     368.9      239.6     1.031    0.991      1.017
  v4k_8tile         8    117.3     71.3    116.8      70.6       34.3     0.608    0.996      0.602
  v4k_8tile_10b     1    508.0    511.8    438.1     437.9      251.1     1.008    0.862      0.862

  trk = 2c7c082 + perf/tracker-halve only;  lr = perf/lr-itx-neon head only.

The two branches are cleanly separable and they do not overlap:

  * the whole of the t=8 multi-tile win is the TRACKER (0.608 alone, 0.602
    composed; lr alone is 0.996 there — inert);
  * the whole of the 10bpc t=1 win is the 16bpc ITX NEON PORT (0.862 alone,
    0.862 composed; trk alone is 1.008 there — the adaptive shift is gated off
    for a serial decode by construction).

They compose without interference. Nothing in this grid shows the two changes
fighting each other.


## THE ONE REGRESSION: single-tile 4K at t=8

  v4k_1tile t=8, ms/frame, per-round values (median of 5, spread < 0.9%):
    base       362.667   [361.667 362.500 362.667 362.833 365.556]
    trk        373.833   [371.500 373.333 373.833 373.889 374.000]   +3.08%
    lr         359.556   [358.556 359.167 359.556 359.667 359.889]   -0.86%
    composed   368.889   [368.500 368.833 368.889 369.167 370.444]   +1.72%
    dav1d_fd1  239.611                                                composed/dav1d = 1.54x

This is REAL, not noise — the arms' per-round ranges do not overlap. The
tracker's adaptive block shift costs 3.08% here and the itx port gives 0.86%
back, netting +1.72% on the composed tree. Mechanism (from the branch report,
and consistent with the attribution above): one tile means the recon task is
serial and the concurrency is post-filter tasks over the same planes, so a
coarser block buys no strided-read locality and only adds shard collisions. The
tracker sees buffer length, not tiling, so it cannot gate itself out.

Worth stating plainly: 1.54x is the BEST t=8 ratio in the whole campaign, so
the cell we made worse is also the cell we are closest on.

  v1024 t=8 is a clean 1.0% WIN (26.72 vs 27.00, tight spreads).
  v256 t=8 is AT THE INSTRUMENT'S RESOLUTION FLOOR and must not be quoted as a
  delta: at ~1.1 ms/frame one millisecond of wall over 18 frames is 0.056
  ms/frame, so the apparent 0.95 is a single quantum, and dav1d's own v256
  column contains a -1.722 ms/frame round (the fit going negative on noise).


