## SOUNDNESS — re-proved by mutation, and one gate was MISSING

`#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` proven ACTIVE, not assumed: an `unsafe` block planted
in `src/safe_simd/itx_arm_hbd.rs` and in `include/dav1d/picture.rs` (each with
`#[allow(unsafe_code)]`, which cannot override `forbid`) fails the build with
E0453 + "usage of an `unsafe` block". Restored, green. The compose adds zero
`unsafe` tokens to rav1d-safe and zero `allow(unsafe_code)` anywhere.

Seven hazards planted in the tracker, each run and restored:

  M1  in-lock `state` re-read deleted from `add`'s
      single-block fast path (the 4af62ae TOCTOU)     DETECTED  wide_exclusion
  M2  `active()` shortened to one shard               DETECTED  narrow_borrow_is_visible_to_wide_registrant
  M3  `publish` does `store(load | bit)` (lost update) DETECTED threaded_churn_leaks_no_slots
  M4  `find` fed an empty occupancy snapshot          DETECTED  7 tests
  M5  consecutive registrants disagree about the
      block boundary by one bit (the adaptive
      shift's premise negated)                        DETECTED  cross_shard_overlaps_are_all_caught
  M6  `retire` does `store(0)` — a release that
      retires every record in the shard, live ones
      included                                        *** NOT DETECTED ***
  M7  registration never publishes its occupancy bit  DETECTED  7 tests

M6 is the failure direction the LOCK-FREE RELEASE introduced and it had no
gate: the whole existing suite (23 lib + 25 soundness + 5 shard_liveness + 1
wide_exclusion) stayed green with live records being silently retired. Closed
by `crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/tests/narrow_release.rs` (commit 2832454), which
puts every record in ONE shard (buffer below `SHARD_MIN_LEN`) and races an
overlapping pair against seven threads of private release churn. It FAILS 3/3
under M6 with 4.2M private grants / 1.15M shared grants / 252k refusals proving
both sides ran, and is green 3/3 restored. With it, all seven mutations are
detected.

Guard EXTENTS: none widened, two narrowed.
  * The block shift changes no extent at all — only which shard an interval
    hashes to.
  * `held-row-guards` (default OFF, measured null) reserves exactly
    `[row_off, row_off + w)` per row, byte-for-byte what the immutable read
    guard and the mutable write-back guard each covered; immutable -> mutable
    only tightens exclusion.
  * The two new per-row kernel guards are byte-identical to the scalar
    references they replace — `src/itx.rs:100-101` and `src/ipred.rs::cfl_pred`
    both already take `dst + y*pxstride` then `slice_mut::<BD>(w)`. They are
    NARROWER than the `block_mut` wide guard the obvious port would have used.
  * The P1 barrier / CDEF / loopfilter guards are untouched.

The 16bpc ITX parity sweep is itself mutation-proven: changing the dct4
constant 1567 -> 1568 fails 4 of the 8 parity arms (both 10bpc small- and
large-coefficient, 12bpc large, and the every-eob arm). `Report::finish`
asserts every cell took the NEON path, so it cannot degenerate into comparing
the scalar reference with itself.

Threading, all green on the composed tree:
  mt_stress (t = 1/2/4/8/16), tile_threading_overlap (3 `--ignored` race
  tests), tile_threading_parity, reproduce_overlap (6 `--ignored`),
  reproduce_overlap_scalar, panic_safety_test, thread_cleanup_test,
  cancellation, managed_api_test, integration_decode (8 `--ignored`),
  worker_panic_recovery (2, under `__test_induce_worker_panic`).

Concurrent multi-decoder pressure — the zenavif#30 wedge shape:
  10 concurrent 4K decoders at t=8, 25 iters each, x 3 vectors x 3 rounds
  = 90 process instances, 2,250 4K frame decodes at ~10x core
  oversubscription. Zero wedges (hard 600 s deadline per group, never
  approached; 14-17 s wall per group) and 90/90 byte-exact CHECKSUMs.
  Harness: ~/tmp/vfy2/concurrent_pressure.sh.


## WHERE THE TWO BRANCH REPORTS WERE WRONG

1. `perf/lr-itx-neon` claims, in a COMMITTED doc header
   (`crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/examples/toctou_stress.rs`), that the 4af62ae
   TOCTOU "has NO regression gate" and that `cargo test -p rav1d-disjoint-mut`
   "still passes everything ... three runs out of three" with the in-lock
   re-read deleted. FALSE at the suite level.
   `wide_exclusion.rs::a_wide_borrow_excludes_every_narrow_shard` FAILS in
   0.03 s — 5 of 5 runs on the composed tree and 3 of 3 on unmodified 2c7c082,
   so it is neither flaky nor an artifact of the tracker change. What survives
   is only the narrow half: the 25 `soundness.rs` tests do miss it, for the
   structural reason that report gives. Header corrected in 2832454. The
   harness is kept as a dev tool; its "needs an in-tracker query hook" close-out
   was reasoning from the false premise and is downgraded to a nice-to-have.

2. `perf/lr-itx-neon` reports "8bpc t=8 is 0.9% slower on the shipped config
   and I did not root-cause it ... two independent sweeps agree on the
   direction". DOES NOT REPRODUCE. Isolated on this box, `lr` alone measures
   116.8 vs base 117.3 at v4k_8tile t=8 — 0.4% FASTER, not 0.9% slower — and in
   the compose it is 70.6 vs the tracker's 71.3 alone, i.e. it takes another
   1.0% OFF rather than adding any back.

3. `perf/tracker-halve` reports its dav1d ratios against the
   PREVIOUSLY-COMMITTED `verify_compose_2026-08-07.meta` dav1d column rather
   than a dav1d arm run alongside its own rows (its report says so; its own
   dav1d arm never produced data). That column's t=8 figures are slow: it cites
   37.4 / 37.9 ms/frame at 8/10bpc where a same-session dav1d measures
   34.7 / 36.3. Its headline "t=8 3.15x -> 2.04x (8bpc), 3.74x -> 2.56x (10bpc)"
   is therefore optimistic; measured against a same-session dav1d the composed
   tree is 2.11x and 2.34x. (The 10bpc figure moves the other way because the
   compose also carries the itx port.) Its ms/frame values themselves reproduce
   well: it claims base 117.9 / 141.6 and newdef 76.3 / 97.1 at t=8; I measure
   116.7 / 137.6 and 73.1 / 85.1 — the 10bpc "newdef" difference is the itx
   port, which its tree did not have.

4. `perf/tracker-halve` reports the single-tile cost as 2.6% (364.6 -> 373.9).
   I measure the tracker alone at +3.08% (362.7 -> 373.8) with non-overlapping
   per-round ranges. Same sign, same mechanism, slightly larger.

5. `perf/lr-itx-neon`'s 10bpc t=1 claim (508.3 -> 455.8 on its own tree; 507.4
   -> 438.4 for its final config) reproduces almost exactly: 508.0 -> 438.1
   isolated, 437.9 composed.


## WHAT IS LEFT, RANKED BY THE SIZE OF THE REMAINING WIN

Absolute ms/frame still owed to dav1d at each cell (composed - dav1d_fd1):

  v4k_8tile_10b t=1   438.1 - 251.3 = 186.8   <- largest single number
  v4k_8tile     t=1   401.0 - 246.4 = 154.6
  v4k_8tile_10b t=2   245.5 - 127.5 = 118.0
  v4k_1tile     t=8   368.9 - 239.6 = 129.3
  v4k_8tile     t=2   216.6 - 125.0 =  91.6
  v4k_8tile_10b t=4   129.9 -  66.8 =  63.1
  v4k_8tile     t=4   116.3 -  65.6 =  50.7
  v4k_8tile_10b t=8    85.1 -  36.3 =  48.8
  v4k_8tile     t=8    73.1 -  34.7 =  38.4

1. SINGLE-THREAD KERNEL THROUGHPUT. t=1 is 1.63x/1.74x and is now the binding
   constraint: it is pure per-thread work with no tracker contention and no
   scheduling, and it caps every other column. If t=1 reached 1.3x and the
   measured 5.48x scaling held, t=8 would be 1.68x — still short, so this is
   necessary but not sufficient. Nobody profiled t=1 on the composed tree; that
   is the first thing the next session should do.

2. THREAD SCALING PAST t=4. t=4 -> t=8 is 0.628/0.655 against dav1d's
   0.529/0.543. Closing that alone is worth ~19% at t=8 (73.1 -> ~59). The
   tracker is no longer the dominant term there (it was; the shift took
   `add_slow` from 72,585 to 23,009 per 6-frame run per the branch's counters),
   so the next contributor has to be re-profiled rather than assumed.

3. LOOP RESTORATION HAS NO NEON TIER AND IS INVISIBLE ON THESE VECTORS. LR is
   switched OFF in both 4K gap vectors, so none of the numbers above contain
   any LR at all — yet the new `md5_inventory --activity` counter shows LR
   active in 696 of 768 corpus vectors, and the branch measured it at 11.5% of
   decode self-time on `10-bit/issues/318_tx_4x4`. This is the largest win that
   the gap grid structurally cannot see. Named starting points are in
   `benchmarks/lr_arm_vs_reference_2026-08-07.meta`.

4. THE SINGLE-TILE REGRESSION (+1.72% composed, +3.08% tracker-alone). Small in
   percent, but it is the only thing this compose made worse, and the fix is
   known in shape: the tracker picks its block shift from buffer length and
   cannot see tiling. Plumbing the tile count (or the observed concurrency
   pattern) to `block_shift_for` would let the coarse shift apply only where it
   pays. Cheap to try, bounded upside (~13 ms/frame).

5. 16bpc ITX ABOVE 16x16 IS UNPORTED. 32- and 64-point transforms and WHT_WHT
   still run the scalar reference. They take zero samples on both 4K gap
   vectors, so this cannot be sized there — find content with the activity
   counters first.

6. `cfl_ac_rust` is still scalar (branch-measured 2.09% self at 8bpc t=1,
   1.54% at 10bpc); the 8bpc small-rect NEON itx kernels
   (`inv_txfm_add_{8x4,4x8,16x8,8x16,16x4,4x16}_8bpc_neon`) exist but are not
   wired into `itxfm_add_dispatch`. Both are small on these vectors and
   plausibly not on rect-heavy content.
